@page "/docs/common/render-modes"

@* Render modes: the three layers every component has, one example under its own mode switch,
   choosing a page's mode, mode support on component pages, and state on static pages. From
   docs/nuget-readme.md, components.md and the [ModeSupport] metadata. *@

<ArticlePage Section="common" Slug="render-modes">
    <Lead>
        The same components work on every page. On a static page they render HTML that works on its own, IzzyBlazor's
        script adds what HTML can't, and on an interactive page the same markup binds to C#.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">Three layers</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem><strong>HTML first.</strong> Every component renders real elements: forms that post, links, radio buttons, native dialogs and selects, with CSS doing the switching. This is all a page has with JavaScript off.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><strong>IzzyBlazor's script.</strong> Small custom elements add keyboard handling, ARIA roles, live values and checks before posting, with no circuit.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><strong>Interactive pages.</strong> On a Server, WebAssembly or Auto page, <Code>@@bind</Code> and event handlers keep C# in step.</ListItem>
        </List>
        <TextBlock>Switch the mode to see one component in each:</TextBlock>
        <ExampleViewer Example="tabs-modes" ModeSwitch />
        <Prose Class="site-table ib-scroll">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th></th><th>JavaScript off</th><th>With IzzyBlazor's script</th><th>Interactive page</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Dialog</td><td>Opens from a trigger or a <code>?dialog=id</code> link; the browser traps focus and Escape closes it</td><td>A dialog opened from the server becomes a real modal; the URL is tidied after closing</td><td><code>@@bind-Open</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Table</td><td>Sorts, filters and pages through the URL</td><td>A select-all checkbox; filters apply as you type</td><td>Sorting, paging, filters and selection bind to C#</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Tabs</td><td>Radio buttons and CSS switch panels</td><td>ARIA tabs, roving focus</td><td><code>@@bind-Selected</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Combobox</td><td>A native select that posts with the form</td><td>Type to filter, keyboard navigation</td><td>Server-side search as you type</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>FileUpload</td><td>The file posts with a <code>Multipart</code> form (<code>IFormFile</code>)</td><td>Drop files on the zone; type and size checked first</td><td><code>OnChange</code> streams <code>IBrowserFile</code>s</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </Prose>

        <Heading Level="2">Choose a page's mode</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            A Blazor Web App's pages are static unless you say otherwise. Make a page interactive where it needs C# on every
            click, and keep the rest static: they need no circuit, so they scale and deploy like plain pages.
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="razor">@Interactive</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            WebAssembly and Auto pages live in the <Code>.Client</Code> project, with <Code>IzzyBlazor.Core</Code>; see
            <Anchor Href="docs/get-started/webassembly-and-auto">WebAssembly or Auto</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">What each component does</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Each component's Overview has a Mode support table, and its API page labels the parameters that need more than
            HTML: <strong>Needs JavaScript</strong>, or <strong>Interactive only</strong> for C# events and
            <Code>@@bind</Code>. Working with JavaScript off and doing more with it isn't labeled: that's every component.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">State on static pages</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            With no circuit, C# runs only when the page is requested, so a static page keeps its state where the browser does:
        </TextBlock>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem><strong>In a form.</strong> <Anchor Href="docs/common/forms">Form</Anchor> posts and validates on the server, and its fields bind to your model.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><strong>In the URL.</strong> <Anchor Href="docs/components/table">Table</Anchor>, <Code>FilterForm</Code> and <Code>Pagination</Code> keep sorting, filters and the page in the query string; <Code>Tabs</Code> takes <Code>QueryKey</Code>, and a dialog opens from <Code>?dialog=id</Code>.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><strong>In the element.</strong> Disclosures, menus, popovers and dialogs open and close in the browser. On an interactive page, <Code>@@bind-Open</Code> reports it to C#, and setting <Code>Open</Code> opens or closes it.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><strong>In a cookie.</strong> The color scheme and the sidebar's state, read on the server so pages arrive right.</ListItem>
        </List>
        <TextBlock>
            Events such as a button's <Code>OnClick</Code> run only on interactive pages. On a static page, use a submit button
            in a form or a link. <Anchor Href="docs/troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</Anchor> has the usual surprises, such as
            <Anchor Href="docs/troubleshooting/state-between-requests">a value that resets on the next request</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Next steps</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/common/forms">Forms and validation</Anchor>: one form for static and interactive pages.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/components">All components</Anchor>: each one's mode support.</ListItem>
        </List>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    private const string Interactive = """
        @page "/orders/{Id:int}/edit"
        @rendermode InteractiveServer
        """;
}
